<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Larissa</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Larissa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Chief city of Thessaly on the Peneios river, seat of the powerful Aleuad family and of the Thessalian League.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Larissa grew on a low hill by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pineios-thessaly">Peneios</a> in the plain of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, inhabited since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. In the Archaic period it became the leading city of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thessalian-league">Thessalian League</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleuadae">Aleuad</a> family, who claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleuas-the-red">Aleuas the Red</a> was credited with organising Thessaly into four districts.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Larissa's landed aristocracy ruled over serfs called penestai and bred horses for cavalry. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleuadae">Aleuad</a> Thorax supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> in 480 BCE. Later Larissa welcomed the sophist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgias">Gorgias</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates">Hippocrates</a> died there. The Aleuads invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon</a> to intervene against the tyrants of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tyranny-of-pherae">Pherae</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 340s BCE Larissa was effectively under Macedonian control. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-quinctius-flamininus">Flamininus</a> proclaimed Greek freedom in 196 BCE it became the seat of the revived <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thessalian-league">Thessalian League</a> and passed under Roman control in 146 BCE; it is a major Greek city today.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/larissa.jpg" alt="Larissa" width="320" height="334"><figcaption>Image: SilentResident (compilation, sources to attribute see above; if any of the sources used in the Montage are removed fr... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Larissa_Montage_L.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Larissa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aleuas, Thorax</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Thessalian Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Larisa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-423&amp;focus=larissa" aria-label="Larissa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5233 758 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Larissa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5553 1014L5553 1014M5553 1014L5548 987"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5553 1014h0M5548 987h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5553 1014h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5553 1014h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-423&amp;focus=larissa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

